Tokens -

    1.  Character level:
        In early days Neural Networks were trained at the character level. Predict the next char in sequence.
        This was much more effective but hardly there were 100 characters and the vocab was created for that 100
        characters.
        Even thought it was small vocab, but predicting each character is hard and expects too much from network.
        Training it also very tough

    2.  Words Level:
        Then Neural networks were trained with words. Predict the next word in the sequence. Much easier to learn from,
        but leads to enormous vocabs with rare words omitted.

    3.  Tokens:
            The breakthrough was to work with chunks of words...
            It's might be a "word", It's might be a fragment of a "word", or it might be "two words".
            We build up a vocab with these fragments of words, as a result we can have a limited sets.
            We can constraint it to set b/c, we go all the way to character level if we needed to.
            We can allow the models to recognise various fragments of words which has some meaning associated with it.
            A middle ground: manageable vocab, useful information to neural network, and elegantly handling
            the word stems - We lean about word stems later.

    Note: Tokens and Vectors are different. We will learn more about vectors in coming modules

